<h2>Why the Quarterback Number Stands Out Too</h2>
<p>Quarterbacks didn't technically set a record in 2026, but their 4.60-second average was the second-fastest ever recorded for the position — and it fits a broader trend rather than a one-year blip. Modern offenses increasingly ask quarterbacks to extend plays, run designed concepts, and threaten defenses as a runner, not just a passer. Combine testing is starting to reflect that shift, with mobility now weighted more heavily in how quarterback prospects are evaluated and drafted.</p>
<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<h2>What This Means for Training Camp</h2>
<p>A faster combine class doesn't automatically mean a faster league — testing speed and game speed aren't identical, and pads change everything. But when the gains are this broad, spanning nearly every position rather than just the usual skill-position sprinters, it's a reasonable signal that the overall athletic bar at the NFL level keeps climbing. Training camp battles this year, particularly along both lines, are worth watching with that context in mind.</p>
